-- title:
--   Proposition 4.11 — every hyperfinite graphical game with bounded payoffs admits a Harsányi $\varepsilon$-equilibrium
-- statement:
--   Let $(X,G,u)$ be a hyperfinite graphical game (bounded payoffs) on a standard Borel space with finite non-empty action set $A$, let $\mu$ be a finite measure on $X$ and $\varepsilon>0$. Then there is a Borel strategy profile $\sigma:X\to\Delta(A)$ with
--   $$\int_X\Big[\max_{a\in A}u(g,(a,\sigma_{-g}))-u(g,\sigma)\Big]\,d\mu(g)\le\varepsilon .$$
--
--   This is the graphical-game version of the main theorem; Lemma 4.6 transfers it back to Bayesian games.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: µ a measure on Ω, used as a probability measure → any finite measure µ; reason: a probability measure is a finite measure
--   - generalization: the vertex set of a graphical game is a Polish space → the vertex set X is a standard Borel space; reason: every Polish space is standard Borel and no notion used depends on the topology of X; the associated game of §4.2 lives on the type space, which is standard Borel without a named Polish topology
--   - encoding: payoff u : Ω × A^Ω → ℝ continuous in the Tychonoff topology on A × A^N(g), extended multilinearly to mixed profiles → u : X → (X → A) → ℝ with each u g continuous on A^X (A discrete) and depending only on g and N(g); the mixed payoff is the integral against the product measure ⊗_h σ(h); reason: given locality, continuity on A^X is continuity on A × A^N(g); the expectation under the product measure is the multilinear extension
--   - encoding: mixed actions in ∆(A) → probability mass functions PMF A; Borel strategies are those with every coordinate g ↦ σ(g)(b) measurable; reason: A is finite, so ∆(A) is the set of probability mass functions on A
--   - encoding: ∫ [gain from deviating] dµ ≤ ε → the gain is µ-integrable and its integral is ≤ ε; reason: in the paper's setting the gain is bounded and measurable, so integrability excludes nothing; it prevents the Bochner default 0 from making the test vacuous
--   - encoding: hyperfinite: generated by a Borel ℤ-action, or alternatively an increasing union of Borel finite equivalence relations (§2.3) → the increasing-union form; reason: the paper states the two forms as equivalent for CBERs (Dougherty–Jackson–Kechris 1994) and its proofs use the increasing-union form
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.11, p.25

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsGraphicalGame
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsHyperfiniteGraph
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsHarsanyiEqG

namespace HellmanLevy.Graphical

/-- **Proposition 4.11.** Every hyperfinite graphical game with bounded payoffs admits a
Harsányi ε-equilibrium: for every finite measure `µ` on the vertex set and every `ε > 0` there
is a Borel strategy profile whose gain from deviating integrates to at most `ε` (4.4).
Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.11, p.25.

**Formalization Note** Boundedness of the payoffs is part of `IsGraphicalGame`. The paper's
measure is a probability measure; any finite measure is allowed here. -/
theorem proposition_4_11 {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] [StandardBorelSpace X] {A : Type*}
    [Fintype A] [Nonempty A] [MeasurableSpace A] [DiscreteMeasurableSpace A]
    [TopologicalSpace A] [DiscreteTopology A] (G : X → X → Prop) (u : X → (X → A) → ℝ)
    (μ : MeasureTheory.Measure X) [MeasureTheory.IsFiniteMeasure μ]
    (hG : IsHyperfiniteGraph G) (hu : IsGraphicalGame G u) (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) :
    ∃ σ : X → PMF A, IsHarsanyiEqG u μ ε σ := by sorry

end HellmanLevy.Graphical
